167 286,182 275,165 271,152 258,145 244,132 238,121 208,119 180,121 150,104 139,82 123,65 132,44 121,33 92,34 79,45 88,62 96,68 88,80 80,91 70,97 51,116 40,130 43,141 33,154" fill="#dfeccf" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M126 83 L136 104 L158 126 L180 143 L208 142 L236 153" fill="none" stroke="#4aa3df" stroke-width="2.5"/><circle cx="42" cy="193" r="7" fill="#e63946" stroke="#111" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="135" cy="246" r="7" fill="#e63946" stroke="#111" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="101" cy="168" r="7" fill="#e63946" stroke="#111" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="105" cy="178" r="7" fill="#e63946" stroke="#111" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="221" cy="153" r="7" fill="#e63946" stroke="#111" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="76" cy="213" r="7" fill="#e63946" stroke="#111" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="140" cy="320" r="7" fill="#e63946" stroke="#111" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="27" cy="177" r="7" fill="#e63946" stroke="#111" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="181" cy="144" r="7" fill="#e63946" stroke="#111" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="76" cy="203" r="7" fill="#e63946" stroke="#111" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="137" cy="84" r="7" fill="#e63946" stroke="#111" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="94" cy="202" r="7" fill="#e63946" stroke="#111" stroke-width="1.5"/></g><text x="110" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bangers" font-size="12" fill="#c0392b">ONE LIGHT</text><text x="340" y="204" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bangers" font-size="12" fill="#ffd60a">TWELVE PLACES</text></svg>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:12px">
    <span class="caption">The story in 5 frames</span>
    <div class="steps" style="margin:0">
      <div><b>The quarrel.</b> Brahmā and Viṣṇu argue over who is supreme.</div>
      <div><b>The pillar.</b> An endless column of fire appears between them. Brahmā flies up as a swan, Viṣṇu digs down as a boar; neither finds its end.</div>
      <div><b>The revelation.</b> Śiva steps out of the pillar: the light without beginning or end is Śiva himself. (Our <a href="/bhakti/shiva/lingashtakam/">Liṅgāṣṭakam</a> article tells it in full.)</div>
      <div><b>Twelve places.</b> The Śiva Purāṇa tells how Śiva later revealed himself as light in particular places, usually to save or bless a devotee: a moon, a mother, a king, a hunted sage. Those places became the twelve jyotirliṅgas.</div>
      <div><b>Seeing the light.</b> Tradition says the light is still there: those far along the spiritual path are said to see the liṅga as a column of light.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="talk"><div class="say z"><svg class="av" viewBox="0 0 64 64" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#kiran"/></svg><p class="bubble"><span class="who">ZAPPY:</span> Why exactly twelve?</p></div><div class="say b"><svg class="av" viewBox="0 0 64 64" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#guru"/></svg><p class="bubble"><span class="who">PROF. BOLT:</span> That's how the Śiva Purāṇa counts them. There are thousands of great Śiva temples, but these twelve hold a special place.</p></div></div>
  <div class="key">One infinite light, <b>twelve places where it touched the earth</b>.</div>
</section>

<section class="page" id="about">
  <h2 class="page-title">ABOUT THE STOTRAM</h2>
  <div class="sub">A prayer that is also a map</div>
  <div class="g2">
    <div class="card bg-blue"><h4>📜 The source</h4><p>The jyotirliṅgas and their legends are told in the <b>Koṭirudra Saṃhitā</b> of the <b>Śiva Purāṇa</b>. The short stotram is traditional; its author isn't known.</p></div>
    <div class="card bg-yellow"><h4>🔢 The shape</h4><p>Three verses name the twelve, four in each. A fourth verse gives the fruit: remembered morning and evening, they destroy the wrongs of seven lifetimes.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="panel reading">
    <p><b>How it's used.</b> Recited on waking and at dusk, before travel, on Mondays and Mahāśivarātri, and by pilgrims planning a <i>dvādaśa jyotirliṅga yātrā</i>, the journey to all twelve.</p>
    <p><b>Longer versions.</b> A longer hymn attributed to Ādi Śaṅkara gives a full verse to each jyotirliṅga. This short one is the version most families learn.</p>
    <p><b>Disputed sites.</b> The stotram uses old names ("Paralī", "Ḍākinī", "Dārukāvana"), and for four of the twelve, more than one temple claims the name. This series covers the most widely visited site and notes the others.</p>
  </div>
</section>

