<section class="page" id="v1b">
  <h2 class="page-title">VERSE 1 · GOING DEEPER</h2>
  <div class="sub">FROM THE WESTERN SEA TO THE NARMADĀ</div>
  <div class="panel reading"><p><b>Somanātha</b> (Prabhās Pāṭan, Gujarat). The Moon, cursed by Dakṣa to waste away, was saved by Śiva here: hence "Lord of the Moon". Destroyed and rebuilt many times through history, it was rebuilt once more after Independence (1951). <b>Mallikārjuna</b> (Śrīśailam, Andhra Pradesh). When Kārttikeya left home in hurt, Śiva and Pārvatī followed him to this mountain and stayed; the temple is also a Śakti Pīṭha.</p><p><b>Mahākāla</b> (Ujjain, Madhya Pradesh). Śiva burst from the earth to destroy a demon troubling the city's devotees, and stayed as "Lord of Time". Famous for its dawn <i>bhasma-ārati</i> with sacred ash, and its south-facing liṅga. <b>Oṃkāreśvara</b> (Māndhātā island, Madhya Pradesh). The island in the Narmadā is said to be shaped like Oṃ; Amaleśvara, "the pure Lord", is honoured with it, on the river's south bank.</p><p><b>The route.</b> These four run from the Arabian Sea inland: coast, Deccan hills, the Mālwā plateau, the Narmadā. Mahākāla and Oṃkāreśvara, both in Mālwā, have long been worshipped as a pair.</p></div>
  <div class="g2"><div class="card bg-yellow"><h4>💡 Somnāth 1951</h4><p>The present Somanātha temple was inaugurated in 1951, with President Rajendra Prasad performing the installation.</p></div><div class="card bg-blue"><h4>💡 Two in Mālwā</h4><p>Ujjain on the Kṣiprā and Oṃkāreśvara on the Narmadā are both in Madhya Pradesh.</p></div></div>
  <div class="inner">Each place is different, sea or hill or river, yet each holds the same light. Divinity isn't limited to one landscape.</div>
  <div class="talk"><div class="say z"><svg class="av" viewBox="0 0 64 64" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#kiran"/></svg><p class="bubble"><span class="who">ZAPPY:</span> Why does the list start with Somanātha?</p></div><div class="say b"><svg class="av" viewBox="0 0 64 64" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#guru"/></svg><p class="bubble"><span class="who">PROF. BOLT:</span> It's traditionally counted as the first jyotirliṅga. And it's on the western sea, so the list starts at India's edge and travels inland.</p></div></div>
  <div class="key">Sea, mountain, ancient city, sacred river: <b>the first four</b>.</div>
</section>
