<section class="page" id="v2b">
  <h2 class="page-title">VERSE 2 · GOING DEEPER</h2>
  <div class="sub">FROM PARALĪ TO THE SEA-BRIDGE</div>
  <div class="panel reading"><p><b>Vaidyanātha.</b> The verse says <i>paralyām</i>, "at Paralī": many take this as <b>Parli Vaijnāth</b> in Maharashtra, while the most widely visited claimant is <b>Baidyanāth Dhām, Deoghar</b>, Jharkhand. Its legend: Rāvaṇa was carrying a liṅga to Laṅkā and was tricked into setting it down; it never moved again. <b>Bhīmaśaṅkara.</b> Most identify it with the temple in the Sahyādri hills near Pune, at the source of the Bhīmā river, where Śiva destroyed the demon Bhīma; a site near Guwahati, Assam, also claims the name.</p><p><b>Rāmeśvaram</b> (Tamil Nadu). On an island near the end of India, Rāma worshipped Śiva before (or, in some versions, after) crossing to Laṅkā; Sītā made a liṅga of sand, and Hanumān brought one from Kailāsa. Its long pillared corridors are among the longest in any Indian temple. No dispute here.</p><p><b>Nāgeśvara.</b> "Lord of serpents", in the Dāruka forest, where the devotee Supriya, imprisoned by the demon Dāruka, kept chanting until Śiva appeared. The best-known site is near <b>Dwārkā</b>, Gujarat, with a giant Śiva statue; <b>Auṇḍhā Nāganāth</b> (Maharashtra) and <b>Jāgeśvar</b> (Uttarakhand) also claim it.</p></div>
  <div class="g2"><div class="card bg-yellow"><h4>💡 Why disputes?</h4><p>The stotram gives old place names, not maps. Over centuries, several temples came to be identified with the same name, and each has its own tradition.</p></div><div class="card bg-blue"><h4>💡 Rāma's Lord</h4><p>Rāmeśvara can mean "Lord of Rāma" or "Rāma's Lord": Rāma himself worshipped Śiva here.</p></div></div>
  <div class="inner">Where there are two claims to holiness, both places are holy to someone. Visit with respect; let the scholars argue.</div>
  <div class="talk"><div class="say z"><svg class="av" viewBox="0 0 64 64" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#kiran"/></svg><p class="bubble"><span class="who">ZAPPY:</span> Some places in this verse are argued about?</p></div><div class="say b"><svg class="av" viewBox="0 0 64 64" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#guru"/></svg><p class="bubble"><span class="who">PROF. BOLT:</span> Yes: four of the twelve have more than one claimant. The stotram gives names, not addresses, and several temples fit them.</p></div></div>
  <div class="key">Healer, warrior, Rāma's Lord, Lord of serpents: <b>the middle four</b>.</div>
</section>
