<section class="page" id="v3b">
  <h2 class="page-title">VERSE 3 · GOING DEEPER</h2>
  <div class="sub">FROM KĀŚĪ TO THE HIMĀLAYA</div>
  <div class="panel reading"><p><b>Viśvanātha</b> (Vārāṇasī, Uttar Pradesh). The city Śiva never leaves, where dying brings liberation; see its full guide in this series. <b>Tryambakeśvara</b> (near Nāśik, Maharashtra). The sage Gautama brought the Godāvarī down here; the liṅga is a small hollow with three tiny liṅgas for Brahmā, Viṣṇu and Śiva.</p><p><b>Kedāranātha</b> (Uttarakhand, about 3,580 m). After the Kurukṣetra war, the Pāṇḍavas sought Śiva, who hid from them as a bull and dived into the ground; his hump remained at Kedār. Open only about six months a year. Ādi Śaṅkara's samādhi shrine stands behind the temple.</p><p><b>Ghṛṣṇeśvara</b> (Verul, near Ellora, Maharashtra). The devotee Ghuśmā made 101 clay liṅgas a day; when her jealous elder sister killed her son, she kept worshipping, and Śiva restored him. The smallest of the twelve temples, restored by the Bhosales and rebuilt in the 18th century by the Holkar queens of Indore; Ahilyābāī Holkar also built Kāśī's present temple.</p></div>
  <div class="g2"><div class="card bg-yellow"><h4>💡 Ahilyābāī's gifts</h4><p>Ahilyābāī Holkar rebuilt or supported temples at several jyotirliṅgas, including Kāśī, Ghṛṣṇeśvara and Somanātha.</p></div><div class="card bg-blue"><h4>💡 Kedāranātha 2013</h4><p>The temple survived the devastating 2013 Uttarakhand floods; a huge boulder lodged behind it is said to have shielded it.</p></div></div>
  <div class="inner">From the busiest city to the remotest valley, the same Śiva waits. Distance is no excuse; neither is crowd.</div>
  <div class="talk"><div class="say z"><svg class="av" viewBox="0 0 64 64" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#kiran"/></svg><p class="bubble"><span class="who">ZAPPY:</span> Kāśī is only ninth?</p></div><div class="say b"><svg class="av" viewBox="0 0 64 64" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#guru"/></svg><p class="bubble"><span class="who">PROF. BOLT:</span> The order follows the verse, not ranking. And it's in good company: Tryambaka, Kedāra, Ghuśmeśa.</p></div></div>
  <div class="key">City of light, river's source, snowy peak, a devotee's faith: <b>the last four</b>.</div>
</section>
<section class="page" id="v4">
  <h2 class="page-title">VERSE 4 · THE FRUIT</h2>
  <div class="sloka" style="margin-top:4px">
    <div class="sloka-head"><span class="theme" style="font-size:22px">MORNING AND EVENING, SEVEN LIFETIMES</span></div>
    <div class="iast"><span class="cl">etāni jyotirliṅgāni</span><span class="cl">sāyaṃ prātaḥ paṭhen naraḥ</span><span class="cl">saptajanmakṛtaṃ pāpaṃ</span><span class="cl">smaraṇena vinaśyati ‖4‖</span></div>
