<section class="page" id="v4b">
  <h2 class="page-title">VERSE 4 · GOING DEEPER</h2>
  <div class="sub">MORNING AND EVENING, SEVEN LIFETIMES</div>
  <div class="panel reading"><p><b>Remembrance.</b> <i>Smaraṇena</i>, "by remembering". The stotram doesn't demand a journey to all twelve; naming them with devotion is itself a pilgrimage of the mind.</p><p><b>Morning and evening.</b> <i>Sāyaṃ prātaḥ</i>: at the two twilights, the traditional times for prayer (<i>sandhyā</i>). Many families recite the twelve names on waking.</p><p><b>Seven lifetimes.</b> <i>Saptajanma-kṛtaṃ pāpam</i>: wrongs done over seven lives. A phalaśruti's large promise; its real point is encouragement to make remembrance a daily habit.</p></div>
  <div class="g2"><div class="card bg-yellow"><h4>💡 Longer versions</h4><p>A longer stotram, attributed to Ādi Śaṅkara, gives one full verse to each jyotirliṅga ("saurāṣṭradeśe viśade'tiramye…").</p></div><div class="card bg-blue"><h4>💡 Śiva Purāṇa</h4><p>The twelve jyotirliṅgas and their stories are told in the Koṭirudra Saṃhitā of the Śiva Purāṇa.</p></div></div>
  <div class="inner">A daily habit of remembrance does what a single grand journey can't. Keep the names close.</div>
  <div class="talk"><div class="say z"><svg class="av" viewBox="0 0 64 64" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#kiran"/></svg><p class="bubble"><span class="who">ZAPPY:</span> Just remembering them is enough?</p></div><div class="say b"><svg class="av" viewBox="0 0 64 64" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#guru"/></svg><p class="bubble"><span class="who">PROF. BOLT:</span> That's the promise: smaraṇena, by remembering. Not everyone can visit all twelve; everyone can name them.</p></div></div>
  <div class="key">Can't travel to all twelve? <b>Name them, morning and evening</b>.</div>
</section>

